## Key Benefits Summary

| Problem | Old Design | New Design |
|---------|-----------|------------|
| **Data Loss** | In-memory state lost on cold start | DynamoDB persists all data |
| **Consistency** | Each container has different state | All containers share same DB |
| **Race Condition** | 10,000 goroutines compete | Single atomic operation |
| **Scalability** | Memory limited per container | DynamoDB/Redis auto-scale |
| **Performance** | 10,000 goroutines = ~100-200ms | Redis INCRBY = <1ms |

---

## Migration Path

**Phase 1: Add Database (no breaking changes)**
```go
// Keep in-memory fallback
if dynamoClient != nil {
    return getAlbumsFromDynamoDB(c)
}
// Fallback to in-memory
c.JSON(http.StatusOK, albums)
```

**Phase 2: Remove In-Memory State**
```go
// Delete these lines:
// var albums = []album{...}
// var albumCounts []albumCount
```

**Phase 3: Seed Initial Data**
```bash
aws dynamodb batch-write-item --request-items file://seed-data.json
```

This approach ensures correct behavior under concurrent Lambda execution while maintaining low latency and high availability.
